course: dictionary
lesson: 13310
title: Grievous: serious and sad
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Grievous is a formal word, so you will meet it more in old stories, news and law than in everyday talk. Context helps. In «The team made a grievous error that cost them the match», the clues are error and cost them the match. Something that costs a team a match is serious, so grievous means very serious.
explain: Grievous can also show deep sadness. In «The town felt a grievous loss», the word loss tells you something precious has gone. Grievous means causing great sadness or pain. Grievous sounds a bit like grieve, which means to feel deep sorrow, and that is a helpful clue.
explain: Grievous has one special meaning in UK law: grievous bodily harm, shortened to GBH. Here the word is part of a crime name. The words around it, such as harm and crime, give you the clue. When you are unsure, swap in very serious and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Grievous» means very serious and harmful, or causing great sadness or pain.
fact: «Grievous bodily harm» (GBH) is a serious crime in UK law of badly hurting someone.
fact: Grievous is a formal word, so it is rare in everyday speech and sounds old-fashioned.
fact: Useful phrases are a grievous error and a grievous loss.
exercise: «The team made a grievous error that cost them the match.» What does grievous mean here? | Happy and lucky | *Very serious and harmful | Quick and easy | Small and silly
exercise: «The town felt a grievous loss when the old lighthouse fell into the sea.» What feeling does grievous show? | Boredom | Great joy | *Great sadness | Surprise at a party
exercise: In UK law, what is «grievous bodily harm» (GBH)? | A type of school test | A small fine for parking | *A serious crime of badly hurting someone | A kind of medicine
test: Which word is closest in meaning to grievous in «a grievous mistake»? | *Serious | Funny | Sleepy | Tiny
test: Why might a writer choose grievous instead of bad? | It means a happy surprise | It is shorter and easier to read | It means bad in a funny way | *It sounds more formal and shows the harm is very serious
exam: What does «grievous bodily harm» mean in UK law? | A small mistake at school | A type of old song | A kind of sports injury | *A serious crime of badly hurting someone
